{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. The positive allowlist, HOME and XDG pointed at a controlled empty location, SUDO_UID neutralised, platform-safe name comparison, a regression per demonstrated vector with a control that fails hard, the false owner-refusal claim corrected, the shared-gitstore mechanism noted and not built. Then review from codex. I will not merge.\n\nWHAT I EXPECT TO BE THE HARD PART, since it is not the allowlist itself. Deciding what is genuinely required is the whole security content of this change, and it is the place where the old habit would reappear: it is very easy to admit a name because something broke without it, and to write the comment as though the necessity had been established rather than observed. So each admitted name gets a stated reason in the comment, and if I cannot say why a command needs it, it does not go in — I will find out what actually fails and say that instead.\n\nPATH IS THE ONE I AM WATCHING. It has to be admitted for exec to find git at all, and it is also an execution channel in its own right: whoever controls PATH chooses which binary named git runs. Admitting it is not obviously safe, it is unavoidable at this layer, and those are different things. I will say which one it is in the comment rather than let the allowlist imply the first.\n\nON THE CONTROLS. Your conditions ask for a control run outside the constructor that fails hard if the exploit stops reproducing. That is the part I would have skipped a week ago and it is what makes the rest trustworthy: a green security regression whose exploit silently stopped working is indistinguishable from a green one whose fix works, and only the control tells them apart. I will make the controls fail rather than skip, so a Git upgrade that closes a vector shows up as a test that needs rewriting rather than as continued reassurance.\n\nON THE CLAIM I HAVE TO CORRECT. The architecture page says a repository owned by another user is refused outright, which is false under sudo. I will make the replacement state the category actually exercised, and I will check the artifact text for the same shape rather than only the page — the sentence in my artifact came from the same reasoning and deserves the same scrutiny.\n\nOne thing I will not do: quietly widen the allowlist to make a test pass. If something legitimate needs a name I did not anticipate, that is a finding about the contract and it goes in the report, not into the list."}